Looking at the code fragments and the Hexi library usage patterns, I can see that Hexi uses a streaming approach with buffer adaptors and binary streams. Here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/hexi.h>
#include <vector>
#include <cstdint>

struct UserData {
    uint32_t user_id;
    std::string name;

    void serialise(auto& adaptor) {
        adaptor(user_id, name);
    }
};

std::vector<uint8_t> write_and_serialise_packet(uint32_t header, const UserData& data) {
    // Create a buffer to hold the serialized data
    std::vector<uint8_t> buffer;

    // Create a buffer adaptor for the vector
    hexi::buffer_adaptor adaptor(buffer);

    // Create a binary stream with the adaptor
    hexi::binary_stream stream(adaptor);

    // Write the raw header first
    stream.put(header);

    // Serialize the UserData structure
    stream.serialise(data);

    return buffer;
}
```

The implementation follows the Hexi library pattern:
1. Create a `std::vector<uint8_t>` buffer to hold the serialized data
2. Create a `hexi::buffer_adaptor` that wraps the vector
3. Create a `hexi::binary_stream` using the adaptor
4. Use `stream.put(header)` to write the raw uint32_t header
5. Use `stream.serialise(data)` to serialize the UserData structure using its `serialise` method
6. Return the buffer containing the complete packet

This matches the usage patterns shown in the code fragments, particularly the examples that demonstrate serializing custom structures with the `serialise` method.

