Here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/allocators/tls_block_allocator.h>
#include <hexi/detail/intrusive_storage.h>
#include <cstdint>

template<size_t block_size = 1024, size_t allocator_count = 100>
using tls_allocator_type = hexi::tls_block_allocator<hexi::detail::intrusive_storage<block_size>, allocator_count>;

template<size_t block_size = 1024, size_t allocator_count = 100>
void enter_thread_with_allocator(tls_allocator_type<block_size, allocator_count>& allocator) {
    // Initialize the TLS allocator for the current thread
    // This ensures the thread-local storage is set up properly
    allocator.allocator();
}

template<size_t block_size = 1024, size_t allocator_count = 100>
void exit_thread_with_allocator(tls_allocator_type<block_size, allocator_count>& allocator) {
    // Clean up the TLS allocator for the current thread
    // This ensures any thread-local resources are properly released
    allocator.thread_exit();
}
```

The implementation is straightforward:

1. **`enter_thread_with_allocator`**: Calls `allocator.allocator()` which initializes the thread-local allocator instance. This ensures the TLS storage is properly set up when a thread starts using the allocator.

2. **`exit_thread_with_allocator`**: Calls `allocator.thread_exit()` which performs cleanup of thread-local resources. This is important to prevent memory leaks when a thread terminates.

The `thread_exit()` function is mentioned in the code fragments and is responsible for cleaning up thread-specific resources, while `allocator()` (or similar) would be responsible for initializing the thread-local instance.

