Looking at the code fragments, I can see that `block_allocator` uses a free list with `push()` and `pop()` methods internally. Since these are private methods, I'll demonstrate their behavior through `allocate()` and `deallocate()` operations.

Here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/allocators/block_allocator.h>
#include <cstdint>
#include <iostream>

// Note: push() and pop() are private, but we can observe their behavior
// through allocate() and deallocate() operations
template<size_t element_count = 10>
void demonstrate_block_allocator_behavior() {
    using namespace hexi;

    // Create a block allocator for uint64_t with specified element count
    block_allocator<std::uint64_t, element_count> allocator;

    std::cout << "=== Block Allocator Free List Demonstration ===\n";
    std::cout << "Allocator created with capacity for " << element_count << " elements\n\n";

    // Demonstrate allocation pattern that shows free list behavior
    std::uint64_t* blocks[element_count];

    // Allocate all blocks initially
    std::cout << "1. Allocating all " << element_count << " blocks:\n";
    for (size_t i = 0; i < element_count; ++i) {
        blocks[i] = allocator.allocate();
        std::cout << "   Allocated block " << i << " at address: " << static_cast<void*>(blocks[i]) << "\n";
    }

    std::cout << "\n2. Deallocating blocks in reverse order (LIFO behavior):\n";
    // Deallocate in reverse order to demonstrate LIFO behavior (push to head)
    for (size_t i = element_count; i > 0; --i) {
        size_t idx = i - 1;
        std::cout << "   Deallocating block " << idx << " from address: " << static_cast<void*>(blocks[idx]) << "\n";
        allocator.deallocate(blocks[idx]);
    }

    std::cout << "\n3. Re-allocating blocks (should reuse in LIFO order):\n";
    // Reallocate - should get blocks in reverse order due to LIFO free list
    for (size_t i = 0; i < element_count; ++i) {
        blocks[i] = allocator.allocate();
        std::cout << "   Re-allocated block " << i << " at address: " << static_cast<void*>(blocks[i]) << "\n";
    }

    std::cout << "\n4. Deallocating alternating blocks to create fragmentation:\n";
    // Deallocate alternating blocks to create a fragmented free list
    for (size_t i = 0; i < element_count; i += 2) {
        std::cout << "   Deallocating block " << i << " from address: " << static_cast<void*>(blocks[i]) << "\n";
        allocator.deallocate(blocks[i]);
        blocks[i] = nullptr; // Mark as deallocated
    }

    std::cout << "\n5. Allocating new blocks (should use free list first):\n";
    // Allocate new blocks - should use the free list entries first
    for (size_t i = 0; i < element_count; i += 2) {
        if (blocks[i] == nullptr) {
            blocks[i] = allocator.allocate();
            std::cout << "   Allocated block " << i << " at address: " << static_cast<void*>(blocks[i]) << "\n";
        }
    }

    std::cout << "\n6. Final cleanup - deallocating all blocks:\n";
    // Clean up all blocks
    for (size_t i = 0; i < element_count; ++i) {
        if (blocks[i] != nullptr) {
            std::cout << "   Deallocating block " << i << " from address: " << static_cast<void*>(blocks[i]) << "\n";
            allocator.deallocate(blocks[i]);
        }
    }

    std::cout << "\n=== Demonstration Complete ===\n";
    std::cout << "This demonstrates how the internal free list works:\n";
    std::cout << "- push() adds blocks to the head of the free list (LIFO)\n";
    std::cout << "- pop() removes blocks from the head of the free list\n";
    std::cout << "- Allocations reuse the most recently freed blocks first\n";
}

// Optional: A simpler version that focuses on the core free list behavior
template<size_t element_count = 5>
void demonstrate_simple_free_list() {
    using namespace hexi;

    block_allocator<std::uint64_t, element_count> allocator;

    std::cout << "\n=== Simple Free List LIFO Demonstration ===\n";

    // Allocate three blocks
    auto* block1 = allocator.allocate();
    auto* block2 = allocator.allocate();
    auto* block3 = allocator.allocate();

    std::cout << "Allocated blocks in order: " << block1 << ", " << block2 << ", " << block3 << "\n";

    // Free in order: 1, 2, 3
    allocator.deallocate(block1);
    allocator.deallocate(block2);
    allocator.deallocate(block3);
    std::cout << "Freed blocks in order: 1, 2, 3\n";

    // Reallocate - should get them in reverse order: 3, 2, 1
    auto* realloc1 = allocator.allocate();
    auto* realloc2 = allocator.allocate();
    auto* realloc3 = allocator.allocate();

    std::cout << "Reallocated blocks: " << realloc1 << ", " << realloc2 << ", " << realloc3 << "\n";
    std::cout << "Expected LIFO order: " << block3 << ", " << block2 << ", " << block1 << "\n";

    // Cleanup
    allocator.deallocate(realloc1);
    allocator.deallocate(realloc2);
    allocator.deallocate(realloc3);
}

// Main function to run the demonstration
int main() {
    demonstrate_block_allocator_behavior<10>();
    demonstrate_simple_free_list<5>();
    return 0;
}
```

This implementation demonstrates the internal free list management of `block_allocator` by:

1. **Showing LIFO behavior**: When blocks are deallocated and reallocated, they follow a Last-In-First-Out pattern, which is characteristic of a singly-linked list where `push()` adds to the head and `pop()` removes from the head.

2. **Visualizing free list operations**: Through allocation and deallocation patterns, we can infer how the internal `push()` and `pop()` methods work.

3. **Demonstrating fragmentation handling**: By deallocating alternating blocks, we show how the free list can become fragmented and still function correctly.

The key insight is that even though `push()` and `pop()` are private, their behavior is clearly observable through the public `allocate()` and `deallocate()` methods, which internally use these free list operations.

