Looking at the code fragments, I can see that the Hexi library provides endian conversion functions. Based on the patterns I see in the fragments, here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/endian.h>
#include <cstdint>
#include <bit>

template<std::endian from, std::endian to, typename T>
T convert_endianness(T value) {
    // Use the library's conditional_reverse function with compile-time endianness
    return hexi::endian::conditional_reverse<from, to>(value);
}

template<typename T>
T convert_endianness_runtime(T value, std::endian from, std::endian to) {
    // Use the library's conditional_reverse function with runtime endianness
    return hexi::endian::conditional_reverse(value, from, to);
}
```

The implementation is straightforward because the Hexi library already provides the necessary functionality:

1. `hexi::endian::conditional_reverse<from, to>(value)` - This is the compile-time version that takes template parameters for the endianness conversion
2. `hexi::endian::conditional_reverse(value, from, to)` - This is the runtime version that takes function parameters for the endianness conversion

Both functions handle the special cases for floating-point types (float/double) by using `std::bit_cast` to convert to integers, byteswap, and then convert back, while arithmetic types use `std::byteswap` directly.

