Looking at the code fragments, I can see that `hexi::static_buffer` is a fixed-size buffer with read and write pointers. Based on the interface shown in the fragments, here's the implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/static_buffer.h>
#include <vector>
#include <cstdint>

template<size_t buffer_size = 1024>
void write_data_to_buffer(hexi::static_buffer<std::byte, buffer_size>& buffer, const std::vector<uint8_t>& data) {
    // Write data to the buffer
    buffer.write(reinterpret_cast<const std::byte*>(data.data()), data.size());
}

template<size_t buffer_size = 1024>
std::vector<uint8_t> read_data_from_buffer(hexi::static_buffer<std::byte, buffer_size>& buffer, size_t bytes_to_read) {
    // Create result vector with requested size
    std::vector<uint8_t> result(bytes_to_read);

    // Read data from buffer
    size_t bytes_read = buffer.read(reinterpret_cast<std::byte*>(result.data()), bytes_to_read);

    // Resize result to actual bytes read (in case buffer didn't have enough data)
    result.resize(bytes_read);

    return result;
}
```

The implementation uses the `write()` and `read()` methods of the `static_buffer` class, which are typical for buffer operations. The `write_data_to_buffer` function writes the entire data vector to the buffer, while `read_data_from_buffer` reads the requested number of bytes (or fewer if the buffer doesn't have enough data) and returns them in a vector.

