Looking at the code fragments, I can see that `hexi::static_buffer` has a `read_ptr()` method that returns a pointer to the current read position, and the buffer maintains separate read and write pointers. The goal is to preview data without advancing the read pointer.

Here's the implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/static_buffer.h>
#include <vector>
#include <cstdint>

template<size_t buffer_size = 1024>
std::vector<uint8_t> preview_buffer_data(const hexi::static_buffer<std::byte, buffer_size>& buffer, size_t bytes_to_preview) {
    // Get the available data size from the current read position
    const size_t available_size = buffer.size() - buffer.read_offset();

    // Limit the preview to available data
    const size_t preview_size = std::min(bytes_to_preview, available_size);

    // Create result vector
    std::vector<uint8_t> result;
    result.reserve(preview_size);

    // Get pointer to current read position
    const std::byte* read_ptr = buffer.read_ptr();

    // Copy data without advancing read pointer
    for (size_t i = 0; i < preview_size; ++i) {
        result.push_back(static_cast<uint8_t>(read_ptr[i]));
