Based on the code fragments and the Hexi library usage patterns, I'll implement the functions for reading and writing containers. The key points are:

1. Writing a container should prefix the element count
2. Reading should first read the count, then read that many elements

Here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/pmc/binary_stream_writer.h>
#include <hexi/pmc/binary_stream_reader.h>
#include <hexi/pmc/buffer_adaptor.h>
#include <vector>
#include <cstdint>

void write_container_to_stream(hexi::pmc::binary_stream_writer& writer, const std::vector<uint32_t>& container) {
    // Write the count first
    writer.write(static_cast<uint32_t>(container.size()));

    // Write each element
    for (const auto& element : container) {
        writer.write(element);
    }
}

std::vector<uint32_t> read_container_from_stream(hexi::pmc::binary_stream_reader& reader) {
    std::vector<uint32_t> container;

    // Read the count first
    uint32_t count = reader.read<uint32_t>();

    // Read each element
    for (uint32_t i = 0; i < count; ++i) {
        container.push_back(reader.read<uint32_t>());
    }

    return container;
}
```

This implementation:
- Uses `writer.write()` to write data to the stream
- Uses `reader.read<T>()` to read data from the stream
- First writes/reads the count as a `uint32_t`
- Then iterates through the elements, writing/reading each `uint32_t`
- Returns the constructed vector in the read function

The code follows the pattern seen in the test fragments where containers are serialized with their size prefixed.

